# WorldNet engine isolation

World chat and village presence talk only to the **engine SPI** (`worldnet/WorldNetEngine`, owned by `WorldNet`). Swapping transport (WebSocket, polling, etc.) must be engine selection only.

## Practical consequences

- No transport-specific type, method, or concept in UI, scene, or gameplay code. Neutral methods on `WorldNet` expose what UI needs (display name, composer disabled, …).
- Capabilities one engine needs become SPI methods — a transport-only public method on a concrete engine is a leak even while only one engine exists.
- Wire/DTO types shared by more than one engine live in a neutral package, not inside one engine's package.
- Chat and presence remain independent streams where possible.

Coexists with [no-pass-through-wrappers.mdc](no-pass-through-wrappers.mdc): the SPI is a real polymorphic seam (allowed); a forwarding-only factory around it is not — selection stays a static on the lifecycle owner.

## RoboVM and third-party realtime SDKs

Mainstream realtime SDKs (Nakama, Ably, Pusher) pull gRPC/Netty/OkHttp/Kotlin/Guava and do not AOT cleanly on RoboVM; presence is often socket-only anyway. For `core`/`SPD-classes` networking prefer `HttpURLConnection`, SSE over that, or a hand-rolled RFC 6455 client over blocking `SSLSocket`. Check transitive deps for those stacks before evaluating a library — see also [cross-platform.mdc](cross-platform.mdc).
